WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.920
During the presidential campaign, Donald
Trump called NATO obsolete. In May, he

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:13.590
imposed a tariff on European steel and
aluminum, ostensibly on national

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.699
security grounds. At the same time, his
administration, like all the previous

00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:21.270
ones since the end of the Second World
War, remains committed to the defense of

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:25.230
Europe. US military deployments in
Eastern Europe have increased in size

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.580
since Trump's inauguration, and perhaps
surprisingly, US sanctions against Russia

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.469
have been stronger than those adopted by
the European Union. As the president is

00:00:34.469 --> 00:00:38.280
headed to the NATO summit in Brussels on
the 11th and 12th of July, the question

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.059
is how those two faces of his
administration can be reconciled. There

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:47.010
are real and legitimate issues at stake.
A free prosperous and secure Europe is

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.730
very much in America's interest, but
the United States cannot care about

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:56.039
European security more than Europeans do.
For a long time, US presidents have tried to

00:00:56.039 --> 00:01:01.469
persuade the Europeans to invest more
into their defense... without success. Maybe

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:04.680
Donald Trump's bluster and abrasive manners
will bring results where diplomacy has

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.000
failed... but don't hold your breath.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.440
What do you think about the role America

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.240
has been playing in NATO? Let us know in
your comments. Also, let us know what

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.850
other topics you'd like our scholars to
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:22.070
and subscribe to more research and
videos from AEI.

